{
  "dom_id": "step-t1367",
  "name": "Expressing a Fraction as a Product",
  "problem": "The usual procedure also works in the reverse direction. For example, to find an equivalent expression for $\\dfrac{2(y+1)}5,$ we can separate the variable part from the rest of the fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(y+1)}{5} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (y+1)}{5\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{(y+1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot (y+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\,(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "",
